WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:06.900
A growth factor binds its receptor, and Ras switches on.

2
00:00:07.160 --> 00:00:09.200
Yet the receptor never touches Ras.

3
00:00:09.800 --> 00:00:14.340
Ras is a small switch protein, anchored to the inner face of the plasma membrane.

4
00:00:15.040 --> 00:00:18.120
The receptor adds phosphates to tyrosines on its own tail.

5
00:00:18.900 --> 00:00:23.140
A phosphate on a tail is a message written where nothing can read it yet, so what

6
00:00:23.140 --> 00:00:24.140
sits in between?

7
00:00:25.700 --> 00:00:30.300
Each phosphorylated tyrosine on the tail is a handhold for proteins that carry an

8
00:00:30.300 --> 00:00:36.720
SH2 domain. An SH2 domain is a small folded module, and it grips a phosphorylated

9
00:00:36.720 --> 00:00:41.020
tyrosine. The cell passes the message on by recruiting these proteins.

10
00:00:41.760 --> 00:00:43.880
Some of the proteins that arrive are enzymes.

11
00:00:44.820 --> 00:00:48.940
Others have no enzyme activity, and only decide who stands next to whom.

12
00:00:49.933 --> 00:00:54.413
One of those proteins, the one with no enzyme activity at all, is Grb2.

13
00:00:54.993 --> 00:00:59.433
It has one SH2 domain, two SH3 domains, and nothing else.

14
00:01:00.173 --> 00:01:04.553
An SH3 domain is another small module that grips short stretches rich in proline.

15
00:01:05.213 --> 00:01:10.613
Its two SH3 domains already hold a protein called Sos, with or without any

16
00:01:10.613 --> 00:01:14.153
phosphate. Only the SH2 domain needs a phosphate.

17
00:01:16.267 --> 00:01:21.527
Now the SH2 domain of Grb2 clamps down on a phosphotyrosine on the receptor tail.

18
00:01:22.247 --> 00:01:25.967
So the receptor holds Grb2, and Grb2 holds Sos.

19
00:01:25.967 --> 00:01:29.447
Sos now stands at the membrane, where Ras is.

20
00:01:30.187 --> 00:01:34.587
So one phosphotyrosine on the tail ends up positioning Sos next to Ras.

21
00:01:34.587 --> 00:01:39.727
Position is the first step, and active Ras then speeds Sos up.

22
00:01:40.650 --> 00:01:42.750
Sos has one task here.

23
00:01:43.270 --> 00:01:47.410
It is an exchange factor that swaps the GDP on Ras for GTP.

24
00:01:47.410 --> 00:01:50.930
That flips Ras from off to on, and no kinase is involved.

25
00:01:51.930 --> 00:01:53.670
Grb2 never grips Ras at all.

26
00:01:54.170 --> 00:01:56.910
It only holds Sos, which acts on Ras.

27
00:01:57.983 --> 00:01:59.923
The receptor never touched Ras.

28
00:02:00.583 --> 00:02:04.423
Grb2 clamped a phosphate on the tail, with Sos already in its grip.

29
00:02:05.143 --> 00:02:07.283
Sos then flipped Ras from off to on.

30
00:02:07.883 --> 00:02:12.603
Grb2 is called an adaptor: it has no enzyme activity, and it links two partners.

31
00:02:13.403 --> 00:02:19.683
Keep this rule: the receptor holds Grb2, Grb2 holds Sos, and position is the first

32
00:02:19.683 --> 00:02:19.983
step.
